.\" fread.3 - binary stream input/output
.TH FREAD 3 "May 2026" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
fread, fwrite \- binary stream input/output
.SH LIBRARY
.B libc
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
.BI "size_t fread(void *" ptr ", size_t " size ", size_t " nmemb \
", FILE *" stream );
.PP
.BI "size_t fwrite(const void *" ptr ", size_t " size ", size_t " nmemb \
", FILE *" stream );
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR fread ()
function reads
.I nmemb
items of data, each
.I size
bytes long, from the stream pointed to by
.IR stream ,
storing them at the location given by
.IR ptr .
.PP
The
.BR fwrite ()
function writes
.I nmemb
items of data, each
.I size
bytes long, to the stream pointed to by
.IR stream ,
obtaining them from the location given by
.IR ptr .
.PP
For both functions the total number of bytes transferred is
.IR "size * nmemb" .
If either
.I size
or
.I nmemb
is zero, no bytes are transferred and the return value is zero; the
state of the stream and the contents of the buffer pointed to by
.I ptr
are unchanged.
.PP
.SS Substrate Implementation
.BR fread ()
first consumes any character previously pushed back with
.BR ungetc (3)
before transferring data from the stream's read buffer.  When the
buffer is exhausted, the implementation refills it via
.BR read (2).
Requests for at least
.B BUFSIZ
bytes after the buffer is empty bypass the stream buffer and read
directly into
.IR ptr ,
which avoids an extra copy for bulk transfers.  A short
.BR read (2)
result that returns 0 sets the end-of-file indicator; a negative
result sets the error indicator.  Either condition terminates the
transfer.
.PP
.BR fwrite ()
copies data into the stream's write buffer and flushes whenever the
buffer fills.  When the stream is in line-buffered mode
.RB ( _IOLBF ),
the buffer is flushed after a newline character is copied in.  When
the stream is unbuffered
.RB ( _IONBF ),
.BR fwrite ()
issues a single
.BR write (2)
of
.I "size * nmemb"
bytes and reports the count converted to elements; partial writes by
the underlying
.BR write (2)
are reflected in the returned element count.
.PP
Both functions report the count of
.I complete
elements transferred.  A partial element at end-of-file or after an
error is not counted, even though its bytes have been read into
.I ptr
(for
.BR fread ())
or written to the stream (for
.BR fwrite ()).
Callers that need to recover from a partial transfer should set
.I size
to 1 and pass the byte count as
.IR nmemb ;
the return value is then the exact number of bytes transferred.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success,
.BR fread ()
and
.BR fwrite ()
return the number of items read or written.  This number equals the
number of bytes transferred only when
.I size
is 1.
.PP
If an error occurs, or the end of the file is reached, the return
value is a short item count, or zero.
.PP
.BR fread ()
does not distinguish between end-of-file and error; callers must use
.BR feof (3)
and
.BR ferror (3)
to determine which occurred.
.PP
A return value of zero from
.BR fread ()
when
.I nmemb
is non-zero indicates that no complete element could be read, either
because end-of-file was reached before
.I size
bytes were available, or because a read error occurred.  Bytes from a
partial element, if any, are left in
.I ptr
but are not reported.
.SH ERRORS
See
.BR read (2)
and
.BR write (2)
for the errors that may be set by the underlying transfer.  The
stream's error indicator is set on any I/O failure and may be tested
with
.BR ferror (3)
and cleared with
.BR clearerr (3).
.SH EXAMPLES
The following program writes an array of integers to a temporary
file, rewinds the stream, reads the data back, and verifies that the
round-trip preserves the contents:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

int
main(void)
{
    int out[] = { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 };
    int in[5] = { 0 };
    size_t n;
    FILE *fp;

    fp = fopen("/tmp/fread.example", "w+b");
    if (fp == NULL) {
        perror("fopen");
        return 1;
    }

    /* Write 5 elements of size sizeof(int). */
    n = fwrite(out, sizeof(int), 5, fp);
    if (n != 5) {
        fprintf(stderr, "short write: %zu of 5\en", n);
        fclose(fp);
        return 1;
    }

    /* Rewind and read back into a fresh buffer. */
    rewind(fp);
    n = fread(in, sizeof(int), 5, fp);
    if (n != 5) {
        if (feof(fp))
            fprintf(stderr, "unexpected EOF after %zu of 5\en", n);
        if (ferror(fp))
            perror("fread");
        fclose(fp);
        return 1;
    }

    /* Verify the round-trip. */
    if (memcmp(in, out, sizeof(out)) != 0) {
        fprintf(stderr, "round-trip mismatch\en");
        fclose(fp);
        return 1;
    }

    /* A trailing partial element is not counted: read 2 more ints
       from a stream positioned at end-of-file and observe that the
       return value is 0, not a fraction. */
    n = fread(in, sizeof(int), 2, fp);
    printf("at EOF, fread returned %zu (eof=%d)\en", n, feof(fp));

    fclose(fp);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
A representative output is:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
at EOF, fread returned 0 (eof=1)
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR read (2),
.BR write (2),
.BR clearerr (3),
.BR fclose (3),
.BR feof (3),
.BR ferror (3),
.BR fopen (3),
.BR fseek (3),
.BR setbuf (3),
.BR ungetc (3)
.SH STANDARDS
.BR fread ()
and
.BR fwrite ()
conform to C89, C99, and POSIX.1-2008.  The convention that the
return value is the number of complete elements transferred, and that
a partial trailing element at end-of-file is not counted, is
specified by C89 \[sc]7.9.8.1 and \[sc]7.9.8.2.
.SH COLOPHON
